/// newspeak-spec.pdf 5.10: a super send starts at "the superclass of the
/// current class C'". For a declaration without a body, its clause
/// messages and arguments are its own code, so C' is the declaration, and
/// a body version's superclass is the level its last clause added (6.2) --
/// the body-less declaration's class object itself. A method of the
/// applied mixin M1 runs with C' the level, so its super starts below M1.
///
/// Each line runs three times, so a cached send answers as the first did,
/// and the two kinds of code share one class object:
///
/// 1. `tag: super foo`: M1's foo, as the body version answers.
/// 2. `tag: [super foo] value`: the same from a block.
/// 3. `tag: super a printString`: M1's slot accessor, nil while the clause
///    runs, before M1's own part initializes it.
/// 4. M1's own `^super foo` on the same instances: Base's foo.
/// 5. The body version of line 1: M1's foo.
class Spec510SuperInABodyLessDeclarationsClauseStartsAtItsClass = Object new () (
  class Base = Object new () (
    public foo = (
      ^'Base foo'.
    )
  )

  class M1 tag: t = Object new (
    |
      public a = t.
    |
  ) (
    public foo = (
      ^'M1 foo'.
    )

    public superFoo = (
      ^super foo.
    )
  )

  class Direct = Base new <: M1 tag: super foo.

  class InBlock = Base new <: M1 tag: [super foo] value.

  class Slot = Base new <: M1 tag: super a printString.

  class WithBody = Base new <: M1 tag: super foo () ()

  public main: platform args: arguments = (
    |
      t = platform transcript.
    |
    1 to: 3 do: [:each |
      t
          show: Direct new a;
          show: ', ';
          show: InBlock new a;
          show: ', ';
          show: Slot new a;
          show: ', ';
          show: Direct new superFoo;
          show: ', ';
          show: InBlock new superFoo;
          show: ', ';
          show: WithBody new a;
          newLine.
    ].
  )
)
